In many respects, IGR J00291+5934 closely resembles the best-known millisecond pulsar, SAX J1808.4-3658. The mass function in IGR J00291+5934 implies a minimum companion mass of M_{c}=0.039M_{\\odot} (for a 1.4M_{\\odot} neutron star and binary inclination i=90^{\\circ}, although the lack of X-ray eclipses or dips implies i\\lesssim 85^{\\circ}). For an isotropic _a priori_ distribution of inclinations, we find an upper limit on the companion mass of 0.16M_{\\odot} (95% confidence, assuming M_{x}=2M_{\\odot}). The inferred mass-radius relation intersects that of a hydrogen main sequence star at a companion mass of 0.24M_{\\odot} (Fig. 4), which requires an improbably small inclination of 10^{\\circ}. This indicates that, like SAX J1808.4-3658, the mass donor in IGR J00291+5934 is a hot brown dwarf, likely heated by low-level X-ray emission during quiescence (Bildsten & Chakrabarty, 2001). For very low-mass companions, the mass transfer rate driven by gravitational radiation is

\\dot{M}_{\\rm GR}\\simeq 3.3\\times 10^{-12}\\left(\\frac{M_{c}}{0.039M_{\\odot}} \\right)^{2}\\left(\\frac{M_{x}}{1.4M_{\\odot}}\\right)^{2/3}\\left(\\frac{P_{\\rm orb }}{2.46\\ {\\rm hr}}\\right)^{-8/3},  where M_{c} is the mass of the donor and M_{x} the mass of the neutron star. Using the long-term averaged flux of 1.8\\times 10^{-11}\\ {\\rm ergs\\,cm^{-2}\\,s^{-1}}, we derive a minimum distance of roughly 4 kpc (for a companion mass of 0.039M_{\\odot}). This would place the system at least 200 pc away from the Galactic plane, likely within the disk. We note that the \\dot{M}_{\\rm GR} above refers to the mass transfer rate averaged over a much longer time scale than the _RXTE_ mission lifetime, so that this distance estimate must be viewed with some caution.

